              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#BoundaryConditions"} Boundary conditions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts_2"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#phenomenology"} Phenomenology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesGauginoCondensation"} Gaugino condensation and supersymmetry breaking
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#generalization_to_mtheory_on_"}
                    Generalization to M-theory on
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mi HW
                          %mo ×
                          %mi ℍ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mi ADE
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1/G_{HW} \times \mathbb{H}/G_{ADE}
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#for_7d_supergravity"} For 7d supergravity
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There is an observation by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten95"} Hořava–Witten 95
            %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten96"} Hořava–Witten 96
            which suggests that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            on an
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group+of+order+2"} Z/2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            (actually a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+orientifold"} higher orientifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 10
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⫽
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{10} \times (S^1 \sslash \mathbb{Z}_2))
            is “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"}> dual
            ” to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"}> fixed point
            ) “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M9-brane"}> M9-brane
            ”. Therefore one also speaks of “heterotic
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Ovrut02"} Ovrut 02
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/M2BraneEndingOnM9.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              from
              %a{:href => "#Kashima00"} Kashima 00
          %p
            In the above the circle factor is taken to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            over the 10d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            If instead one considers another of the spatial dimensions to be compactified on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⫽
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1\sslash \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"}> F-theory
            ) result is supposed to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} type I string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M9-brane"} M9-brane
            (the latter now wrapping the M-theory circle fiber) is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-string"} E-string
          %p More in detail:
          %p
            One considers the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            on a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group+of+order+2"} Z/2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circles"} circles
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi S
                            %mi A
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo ×
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi S
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mtext radius
                          %mo :
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 11
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; S^1_A &amp;\times&amp; S^1_B
                  \\\
                  \\text{radius}: &amp;  R_{11} &amp;&amp; R_{10}
                  }
          %p
            such that the reduction on the first factor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A
            corresponds to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            along the second factor yields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB string theory
            (in its
            = succeed "-incarnation)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi IIB
                  %mover
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi S
                  %mi IIB
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  IIB \overset{S}{\leftrightarrow} IIB
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/HoaravaWittenCompactifications.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics taken from
              %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten95"} Horava-Witten 95, p. 15
          %p
            If one considers this situation additionally with a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            quotient of the first circle factor, one obtains the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+heterotic+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and heterotic string theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horava-Witten+theory"}> Horava-Witten theory
            ). If instead one performs it on the second circle factor, one obtains
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} type I string theory
          %p
            Here in both cases the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            is by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflection"} reflection
            of the circle at a line through its center. Hence if we identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \simeq \mathbb{R} / \mathbb{Z}
            then the action is by multiplication by /1 on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          %p In summary:
          %p M-theory on
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi A
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⫽
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi B
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S^1_A \sslash \mathbb{Z}_2 ) \times S^1_B
                yields
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi A
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msubsup
                          %mi S
                          %mi B
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⫽
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_A \times \left( S^1_B \sslash \mathbb{Z}_2 \right)
                yields
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I%27+string+theory"} type I' string theory
          %p
            Hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            that swaps the two circle factors corresponds to a duality between heterotic E and type I’ string theory. And T-dualizing turns this into a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+type+I+and+heterotic+string+theory"} duality between type I and heterotic string theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi HE
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ↔
                            %mrow
                              %mi KK
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %msubsup
                                %mi ℤ
                                %mn 2
                                %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ↔
                            %mrow
                              %mi KK
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %msubsup
                                %mi ℤ
                                %mn 2
                                %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                          %mo ′
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↕
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↕
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mi T
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi HO
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ↔
                            %mrow
                              %mphantom
                                %mi A
                              %mi S
                              %mphantom
                                %mi A
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  HE
                  \&amp;\overset{KK/\mathbb{Z}^A_2}{\leftrightarrow}&amp;
                  M
                  \&amp;\overset{KK/\mathbb{Z}^B_2}{\leftrightarrow}&amp;
                  I'
                  \\\
                  \\mathllap{T}\updownarrow
                  \&amp;&amp;  &amp;&amp;
                  \\updownarrow \mathrlap{T}
                  \\\
                  HO
                  \&amp;&amp; \underset{\phantom{A}S\phantom{A}}{\leftrightarrow} &amp;&amp;
                  I
                  }
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/HoravaWittenCompactificationsII.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics taken from
              %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten95"} Horava-Witten 95, p. 16
          %h3#duality_between_mtheory_and_heterotic_string_theory Duality between M-theory and heterotic string theory
          %p
            Here each of the two copies of the heterotic gauge theory is a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+sector"}> hidden sector
            ” with respect to the other.
          %p
            The orbifold equivariance condition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            is that discussed at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            (there for the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            = succeed "-action." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [2G_4] = 2 [c_2] - [\frac{1}{2} p_1]
          %p
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            becomes the condition for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+mechanism"} Green-Schwarz mechanism
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            on the “boundary” (the orbifold fixed points).
          %h3#duality_between_mtheory_and_type_i_string_theory Duality between M-theory and type I string theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+I+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type I string theory
          %p
            originally suggested in (
            %a{:href => "#HoravaWitten95"}> Horava-Witten 95, section 3
            )
          %p
            Further evidence is reviewed in
            %a{:href => "#APT98"} APT 98
          %h3#duality_between_heterotic_and_type_i_string_theory Duality between heterotic and type I string theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+heterotic+and+type+I+string+theory"} duality between heterotic and type I string theory
          %p
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            in the
            = succeed "-picture\u2026" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+F-theory+and+heterotic+string+theory"} duality between F-theory and heterotic string theory
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+a+circle"} M-theory on a circle
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+8-manifolds"}> M-theory on 8-manifolds
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+Spin%287%29-manifolds"} on Spin(7)-manifolds
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+Sp%281%29-manifolds"} M-theory on Sp(1)-manifolds
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+hyperbolic+manifolds"} M-theory on hyperbolic manifolds
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+M-theory+on+ADE-orbifolds"} heterotic M-theory on ADE-orbifolds
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} M-theory on Calabi-Yau manifolds
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%2FM-theory+on+elliptically+fibered+Calabi-Yau+2-folds"} on CY2-folds
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%2FM-theory+on+elliptically+fibered+Calabi-Yau+3-folds"} on CY3-folds
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%2FM-theory+on+elliptically+fibered+Calabi-Yau+4-folds"} on CY4-folds
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%2FM-theory+on+elliptically+fibered+Calabi-Yau+5-folds"} on CY5-folds
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#BoundaryConditions Boundary conditions
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat G_4
            is supposed to vanish, and
            %em differentially
            vanish at the boundary in the HW model, meaning that also the local connection 3-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_3
            vanishes there. The argument is roughly as follows (similar for as in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Falkowski"} Falkowski, section 3.1
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher Chern-Simons term
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C_3 \mapsto C_3 \wedge G_4 \wedge G_4
          %p
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            is supposed to be well-defined on fields on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            and hence is to be
            = succeed "-invariant." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mn 11
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota_{11}
            be the canonical vector field along the circle factor. Then the component of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \wedge G
            which is annihilated by the contraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mn 11
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota_{11}
            is necessarily even, so the component
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 11
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mn 11
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d x^{11}\wedge \iota_11 C_3
            is also even. It follows that also
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 11
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mn 11
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d x^{11}\wedge \iota_11 G_4
            is even.
          %p Moreover, the kinetic term
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C \mapsto G \wedge \star G
          %p
            is to be invariant. With the above this now implies that the components of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            annihiliated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mn 11
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota_{11}
            is odd, because so is the mixed component of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            tensor.
          %p
            This finally implies that the restriction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_3
            to the orbifold fixed points has to be closed.
          %h2#related_concepts_2 Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+F-theory+and+heterotic+string+theory"} duality between F-theory and heterotic string theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M9-brane"} M9-brane
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The original articles are
          %ul
            %li#HoravaWitten95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Petr+Ho%C5%99ava"}> Petr Hořava
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Heterotic and Type I string dynamics from eleven dimensions
                %strong 460
                (1996) 506 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9510209"}> arXiv:hep-th/9510209
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(95)00621-4"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(95)00621-4
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong Coupling Expansion Of Calabi-Yau Compactification
                %strong 471
                (1996) 135-158 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(96)00190-3"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(96)00190-3
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9602070"} arXiv:hep-th/9602070
            %li#HoravaWitten96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Petr+Ho%C5%99ava"}> Petr Hořava
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Eleven dimensional supergravity on a manifold with boundary
                %strong 475
                (1996) 94 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(96)00308-2"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(96)00308-2
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9603142"} arXiv:hep-th/9603142
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Piyush+Kumar"}> Piyush Kumar
                ,
                %em Hořava-Witten theory
                (2004) (
                %a{:href => "http://theory.uchicago.edu/~sethi/Teaching/P484-W2004/hwitten.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Townsend"}> Paul Townsend
                ,
                %em Four Lectures on M-Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9612121"}> arXiv:hep-th/9612121
                ).
            %li#Ovrut02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burt+Ovrut"}> Burt Ovrut
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on Heterotic M-Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strings, Branes and Extra Dimensions
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0201032"} arXiv:hep-th/0201032
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789812702821_0007"} doi:10.1142/9789812702821_0007
            %li#Falkowski
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adam+Falkowski"}> Adam Falkowski
                , section 3 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Five dimensional locally supersymmetric theories with branes
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/FalkowskiLecture.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Carlevaro06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luca+Carlevaro"}> Luca Carlevaro
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Three approaches to M-theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://libra.unine.ch/handle/123456789/16186"} hdl:123456789/16186
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://libra.unine.ch/server/api/core/bitstreams/b5eedf5f-8029-4700-83cb-806d0737a669/content"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/1253257"} spire:1253257
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            solution in HW-theory, supposedly yielding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            at the intersection with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M9-brane"} M9-brane
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#LalakLukasOvrut97
              %p
                Zygmunt Lalak,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Lukas"} André Lukas
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burt+Ovrut"} Burt Ovrut
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Soliton Solutions of M-theory on an Orbifold
                %strong 425
                (1998) 59-70 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9709214"}> arXiv:hep-th/9709214
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Kashima00
              %p
                Ken Kashima,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    The M2-brane Solution of Heterotic M-theory with the Gauss-Bonnet
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi R
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^2
                    terms
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0010286"} arXiv:hep-th/0010286
          %p
            More on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+mechanism"} Green-Schwarz mechanism
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%C5%99ava-Witten+theory"} Hořava-Witten theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                E. Dudas, J. Mourad,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the strongly coupled heterotic string
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9701048"} arXiv:hep-th/9701048
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adel+Bilal"}> Adel Bilal
                , Jean-Pierre Derendinger, Roger Sauser,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    M-Theory on
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %msub
                              %mi ℤ
                              %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1/\mathbb{Z}_2
                %strong 576
                (2000) 347-374 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912150"}> arXiv:hep-th/9912150
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Ian G. Moss:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Boundary terms for supergravity and low energy heterotic M-theory
                %strong 729
                1–2 (2005) 179-202 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2005.09.023"}> doi:10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2005.09.023
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Ian G. Moss,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A new look at anomaly cancellation in heterotic M-theory
                %strong 637
                (2006) 93-96 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0508227"}> arXiv:hep-th/0508227
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Sergio Lukic,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Moore"} Gregory Moore
                %em Flux corrections to anomaly cancellation in M-theory on a manifold with boundary
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0702160"}> arXiv:hep-th/0702160
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Ian G. Moss,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher order terms in an improved heterotic M theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.1662"} arXiv:0810.1662
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fei+Han"}> Fei Han
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kefeng+Liu"} Kefeng Liu
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weiping+Zhang"} Weiping Zhang
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Anomaly Cancellation and Modularity. II:
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 8
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 8
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8 \times E_8
                    case
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.4540"} arXiv:1209.4540
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-016-9034-1"} doi:10.1007/s11425-016-9034-1
          %p
            Explicit discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-branes"} M2-branes
            ending on the HW fixed points and becoming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+strings"} heterotic strings
            is discussed, via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BLG+model"} BLG model
          %ul
            %li#Lambert15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Neil+Lambert"}> Neil Lambert
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Heterotic M2-branes
              %strong 749
              (2015) 363-367 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1507.07931"}> arXiv:1507.07931
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            After
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-reduction"} KK-reduction
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/5d+supergravity"} 5d supergravity
            there is a corresponding 5d mechanism, see the references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "5-dimensional+supergravity#ReferencesHWCompactification"} there
          %p
            Discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+heterotic+and+type+I+string+theory"} duality between heterotic and type I string theory
            includes
          %ul
            %li#APT98
              I. Antoniadis, H. Partouche, T. R. Taylor,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on Heterotic-Type I Duality
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mirjam+Cveti%C4%8D"}> Mirjam Cvetič
              , Max Wiesner:
              %em Non-perturbative Resolution of Strong Coupling Singularities in 4d N=1 Heterotic/M-theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12458"}> arXiv:2408.12458
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#phenomenology Phenomenology
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            in Horava-Witten theory:
          %ul
            %li#Ovrut18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burt+Ovrut"}> Burt Ovrut
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vacuum Constraints for Realistic Heterotic M-Theories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08892"} arXiv:1811.08892
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.3390/sym10120723"} doi:10.3390/sym10120723
          %p
            Discussion of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+M-theory"} heterotic M-theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %ul
            %li#DOPW99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ron+Donagi"}> Ron Donagi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burt+Ovrut"} Burt Ovrut
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Pantev"} Tony Pantev
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Waldram"} Daniel Waldram
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Standard Models from Heterotic M-theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912208"} arXiv:hep-th/9912208
            %li#DOPW00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ron+Donagi"}> Ron Donagi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burt+Ovrut"} Burt Ovrut
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Pantev"} Tony Pantev
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Waldram"} Daniel Waldram
                = succeed "," do
